  "rationale": "The defining statement of the postwar Count Basie Orchestra \u2014 the 1957 Neal Hefti book that relaunched the band on Roulette and fixed its late-'50s sound. Consensus across independent traditions: 1001 Albums You Must Hear Before You Die; Colin Larkin's All Time Top 1000 Albums (#411); Grammy Best Jazz Performance Group AND Best Performance by a Dance Band at the 1st Annual Grammys; Penguin Guide 4 stars; AllMusic 5 stars (Eder: 'a bold, forward-looking statement within the context of a big-band recording'). Lineage: the same Basie/Hefti/Reig unit immediately cut Basie Plays Hefti, and 'Li'l Darlin'' became a big-band standard. Big-band era representative alongside the Ellington and Kenton records already in the canon. Style tagged post-bop following the Ellington big-band precedent (no swing/big-band code exists in the style table); the boundary is the late-50s big band at the edge of the post-bebop tradition.",
