    "notes": "Two-day session (Oct 21-22, 1957) at Capitol Studios New York \u2014 Studio A, Eaves Building, 151 West 46th Street. All 11 tracks on the original 1958 release are Neal Hefti compositions/arrangements. Reissue (The Complete Atomic Basie, 1994) adds Jimmy Mundy arrangements (Silks and Satins, Sleepwalker's Serenade) and The Late Late Show incl. Joe Williams vocal \u2014 reissue-only, excluded here. Engineer not credited in the sources consulted (Capitol house engineer, undocumented) \u2014 null. AllMusic credits list 'Jo Jones' nowhere but list Billy Mitchell (tenor) \u2014 Mitchell not in Wikipedia's personnel; treated as unverified/unknown presence, excluded from personnel array (AllMusic may aggregate band personnel across the Roulette era). Track sides per 1958 LP; 'Whirlybird' written 'Whirly-Bird' in AllMusic. Arranger Neal Hefti (all 11 tracks) is a production credit, not a performing musician; kept out of the personnel array."
  },
  "ballot": {
    "album": "Count Basie \u2014 The Atomic Mr. Basie (1958)",
    "scope_check": {
      "in_window": true,
      "boundary_note": "Whether a 1950s big-band record whose organizing idiom is ensemble swing \u2014 not the harmonic/structural explorations of post-bop \u2014 belongs in a post-bebop canon, given that Ellington and Kenton big-band records are already in. The 'post-bop' style tag is a workaround for the absence of a big-band code, not a description of the idiom."
    },
    "tier": "scope_call",
    "priority": "strong",
    "case_for": "The defining statement of the postwar Basie Orchestra \u2014 the 1957 Neal Hefti book that relaunched the band on Roulette and fixed its late-'50s sound \u2014 with consensus across independent traditions: 1001 Albums You Must Hear Before You Die, Larkin's All Time Top 1000 (#411), AllMusic 5 stars, and a rare double win at the 1st Annual Grammys. Session gravity is exceptional for a big-band date: Thad Jones (trumpet) bridges to the Thad Jones/Mel Lewis Orchestra, a load-bearing post-bop big-band lineage node, and the sections are stacked with post-bebop network voices \u2014 Frank Foster, Frank Wess, Eddie 'Lockjaw' Davis, Snooky Young, Joe Newman. 'Li'l Darlin'' became a permanent big-band standard, and the record still plays as perhaps the most accessible entry point to large-ensemble jazz. Alongside the Ellington and Kenton records already in the canon, a Basie anchor is the missing third leg of the postwar big-band stool.",
    "case_against": "It is fundamentally a swing record \u2014 it literally won the Grammy for 'Best Performance by a Dance Band' \u2014 and its organizing principle is tight, riff-based ensemble swing, not the harmonic or structural explorations of hard bop, modal, or post-bop. If the canon's mandate is to trace the post-bebop language specifically, this is a glorious continuation of the swing era rather than a load-bearing pillar of that lineage; the 'post-bop' style tag is a workaround for the absence of a big-band code, not a description of the idiom. The dossier's heaviest consensus claims (Penguin 4 stars, Larkin #411, the Grammy wins) also appear in the rationale text without discrete source URLs \u2014 the source set verifies personnel and session data but not the Penguin or Larkin citations.",
    "disagreement": "The references split on tier and scope: one reads the Basie/Hefti band as inside the post-bebop tradition (consensus_core, must_have) on the strength of modern arrangements, post-bebop soloists, and the Thad Jones lineage bridge; the other reads it as a swing-era continuation whose organizing idiom tests the post-bebop boundary (scope_call, strong), noting it won a Grammy for 'Best Performance by a Dance Band.'",
    "recommendation": "The council proposes John rule on whether the Ellington/Kenton big-band precedent extends to the 1957 Basie/Hefti band: if yes, this is a consensus_core must-have anchor; if the canon's center of gravity excludes dance-band swing as an organizing principle, it falls out of scope.",
